## R-002 — Windows Defender Signatures Stale (Root Cause: Network Segmentation)

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Asset** | DC01 (domain controller) |
| **Identified** | July 16–17, 2026 |
| **Status** | In Remediation |
| **Finding** | Windows Defender antimalware signatures had not updated in over 3 days at time of scan. Root cause traced directly to the lab's own network segmentation: DC01 sits in a segment with no outbound internet access by design, so Defender cannot reach Microsoft's update servers. |
| **Severity** | High |
| **Likelihood** | Medium — stale signatures don't create an active exploit path directly, but meaningfully reduce detection of newer malware variants |
| **Impact** | High — a core endpoint defense control is degraded across every host in the affected segment, not just this one machine |
| **Treatment** | **Remediate** — extend the same controlled-egress pattern already built for Linux hosts (a dedicated internal package proxy) to Windows: either a scoped, temporary outbound allow rule during defined patch windows, or an internal WSUS-style update server on the monitoring segment |
| **Residual Risk (post-treatment)** | Low — a controlled, logged update path restores detection currency without reopening standing internet access to the segment |
| **Next Review** | Upon remediation completion; re-verify signature currency monthly thereafter |
| **Framework Mapping** | NIST CSF: PR.MA-1 (Maintenance) · DE.CM-4 (Malicious Code Detection) · CIS Control 10 (Malware Defenses) |
| **Note** | This finding is a direct, real consequence of a deliberate architectural decision (segmentation), not an oversight — identified while running a routine vulnerability scan, and tracked as a genuine trade-off between security posture and maintainability rather than something to quietly patch around. |

