## R-003 — Self-Signed Certificate on Firewall Management Interface

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Asset** | Network firewall/router — internal management interface |
| **Identified** | July 16–17, 2026 |
| **Status** | Accepted |
| **Finding** | The management GUI uses a default self-signed certificate, not signed by a recognized Certificate Authority. |
| **Severity** | Medium (CVSS 6.5) |
| **Likelihood** | Low — exploitation would require an attacker already positioned on the internal network with the ability to intercept traffic to this specific internal management interface |
| **Impact** | Low, in this context — this is a private, internal-only management interface, not a public-facing production service |
| **Treatment** | **Accept** — documented rationale: the cost of a purchased certificate or standing up an internal Certificate Authority is not justified by the realistic risk to an internal-only, isolated-network management interface |
| **Residual Risk (accepted)** | Low — bounded by network isolation; no plan to remediate unless the interface's exposure changes |
| **Next Review** | Annually, or immediately if this interface is ever exposed beyond the current isolated segment |
| **Framework Mapping** | NIST CSF: ID.RA-1 (Risk Assessment) — demonstrates the finding was evaluated and consciously accepted, not overlooked |

